WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.136
Almost every report asks:

2
00:00:02.136 --> 00:00:03.473
when, and how long?

3
00:00:03.473 --> 00:00:08.150
In this lesson, you will learn to filter a page with a date slicer,

4
00:00:08.150 --> 00:00:13.830
show the current date with NOW and TODAY, and measure the days between two dates with DATEDIFF.

5
00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:15.500
Let's get on the clock!

6
00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:20.631
On this page, a date slicer is built from the calendar.

7
00:00:20.631 --> 00:00:23.660
I format it so the dates are easy to read.

8
00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:34.020
Change the start and end dates, and every visual on the page updates. Try it!

9
00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:46.117
The card on the right shows the current date and time with NOW.

10
00:00:46.117 --> 00:00:49.360
TODAY works the same way, but returns only the date.

11
00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:57.860
In Model view, here are the tables we will use.

12
00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:07.720
The Customers table holds customer details.

13
00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:17.620
And the Orders table has three key dates:

14
00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:20.000
order date, required date, and shipped date.

15
00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:31.419
A card with NOW shows when the report was refreshed. Now our goal:

16
00:01:31.419 --> 00:01:34.200
count the days between two dates, with DATEDIFF.

17
00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:08.493
I create a measure called Date Interval, and type DATEDIFF.

18
00:02:08.493 --> 00:02:09.930
It needs three arguments:

19
00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:14.960
the first date, the second date, and the unit, like DAY, MONTH, or YEAR.

20
00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:30.951
Leave one out, and Power BI warns you: too few arguments.

21
00:02:30.951 --> 00:02:33.160
So we always give all three.

22
00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:51.855
We want the difference for each order, between the order date and the required date.

23
00:02:51.855 --> 00:02:57.140
This shows how much time each order had, and which ones were late. Great job today!

